.get('outcome'))"); case $DO in quiet) T6=ok; echo "test 6: harness exit 0 and the recorder's cleanup done, run directory sealed, the drain quiet (manifest drain.outcome)";; gave-up) T6=gaveup; stop "test 6: the drain gave up (manifest drain.outcome 'gave-up'): a failed recovery, retained in the sealed run as a valid observation - no delta on this run";; *) stop "test 6: the harness exited 0 but the manifest's drain outcome is '$DO' - do not continue";; esac; else stop "test 6: the harness run was not sealed, or it exited $HR (the [harness] INVALID lines above name the reasons, a drain that ended in error among them; 2: the harness was not started - a recorder cleanup that failed on the way is named in the STOP of events_start or harness_cmd above, and the unit may then still run) - do not continue"; fi; else stop "test 6: $RID refused as already used (F6='$F6'), no seed, not ready, not drained or no configuration identity - the harness run was NOT started"; fi     # F6 first: an entry the first line refused starts nothing here; the plan's derived seed, not 42: these are other devices; the configuration identity is captured on the guest by config_identity (6.1) BEFORE the harness starts and handed to it with --config-identity-from, so the manifest embeds it (an incomplete file is refused by the harness: an INVALID line, exit 1 here); the twin hook takes each snapshot as the runbook's '$REC snap' ('after' --like before) and writes it first as the write-once sibling $P/$RID.twins.<label>.json, which delta reads through --prefix; the drain hook's output is the drain transcript (logs/sut/hook-drain.stdout.txt and .stderr.txt), which the harness classifies: drain.outcome quiet, gave-up (a failed recovery the harness retains, the fetches still after it) or error (an INVALID line); T6 is ok only when harness_cmd answered 0 - the harness's run valid AND the recorder's cleanup done - on a sealed run whose drain was quiet; a cleanup that failed (3) is its own state, never a tolerated harness status; the StartedAt read of decision 1a (adopted 2026-09-30) is the harness's own --fetch-started-at-cmd, run right after its docker-events fetch and before it ingests the resources: fetch_started_at.sh reads egw-controller-1's id and State.StartedAt on the guest into logs/sut/controller-started-at.txt (a read that failed is an INVALID line, exit 1 here), and the harness judges the controller's resource gap across this restart by the proved-down interval only when the complete capture (one die and one start of one container id) and that read (StartedAt within 1 s of the start) establish it - otherwise by the ordinary 5 s rule; this line, after the harness, prints the manifest's resources_proved_down read-only (applies or why not, D, S, E, both edge gaps, the rows rejected) and decides nothing, and when harness_cmd answered 2 (the harness not started: a run directory already there is another execution's) it reads no manifest; UNVERIFIED: fetch_started_at.sh on the real guest (Appendix B, item 24)
